#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Run the compatibility rows of one runner in sequence and guard what each
row could leave behind for the next.

usage: run-rows          (run from the repository root; everything via env)
env:   ROWS               space-separated <runtime>:<target> words, run in order
       ROW_LIMIT_SECONDS  wall-clock limit of one row
       OUT_DIR            receives the attestation-*.json and junit-*.xml files
                          of scripts/release-workflow/run-row and the results
                          file row-results.jsonl
       VERSION, COMMIT, DAEMON_DIR, PACKAGES_DIR, CONSUMER, XTASK
                          passed on to run-row unchanged

Every row runs run-row in its own session and process group, with a fresh,
empty, owner-only root (ROW_ROOT) that this script creates and removes. The
rows of one runner share the runner's account, so after each row this script
checks that the row left nothing behind that a later row could see:

- no process whose executable, working directory or environment points into
  the finished row's root, which also holds the consumer's TestEnv roots, or on
  macOS into a test root the row created under /private/tmp (MACOS_TEMP_ROOT
  of the test-support crate). A survivor is killed and fails the row;
  only its pid, executable and the matched variable name are reported, never
  an environment value or a command line;
- no row root and no new test root left on disk;
- no unit of the systemd user manager or job of the launchd user domain added
  or removed, wherever systemctl or launchctl answers. macOS loads agents on
  demand whatever a row does (Spotlight workers, the Books and Podcasts
  agents), so a launchd job does not count when it is a named transient
  Spotlight worker, or when it was added and its program lives on the sealed
  system volume (/System), which a row cannot write; the results name such
  jobs, and the job log names every added job with its program path alone,
  looked up in the process's, the login session's and the user's domain;
- no item added to or removed from the login keychain, wherever `security`
  answers (macOS), compared as
  a multiset of class, label, service, account and server from
  `security dump-keychain` without `-d`, which reads attributes only; a change
  is reported by class and label alone;
- the manifest of everything a later row trusts is unchanged: xtask, the
  consumer, run-row and this script, the downloaded archives, the attestations
  and reports of earlier rows, the Node, uv and Python installs on PATH and
  where each resolves, the commands the guards, run-row and the upstream
  staging resolve through PATH (path and content), the entries of every PATH
  directory, and the checkout. The checkout's tracked files are
  listed once by `git ls-files` before the first row and hashed directly, so
  no change hides behind git's index; the entries of every directory holding
  them and git's configuration, hooks, info and refs are recorded too. A
  mismatch stops the job at once and names the row that ran last. The guards
  run their commands by the paths resolved before the first row, and run git
  only before it.

Every row also starts without the `__pycache__` entries of the tool installs
and of the checkout's directories: the manifest leaves bytecode caches out,
because the rows' Python helpers write them, so they are removed instead and
no bytecode a row wrote runs in a later row.

A failing row never stops the rows after it, except when it changed the
account (a unit, job or keychain item added or removed), which the next row
would take as its starting state, or when the guards cannot check a row that
ran (a guard command fails after the row started, an account guard answers on
only one side of the row, or a bytecode cache cannot be removed): then the
isolation of the next row is unknown, and the job stops as for a mismatch. Each row's result goes to the results file, to an error
annotation and to the step summary; the script exits non-zero when any row did
not pass.

Threat model. The guards catch accidental damage one row's upstream or
consumer run does to the rows after it: a process or service left running, a
keychain item, a changed file, a shadowed command. They do not stand against
an upstream that deliberately evades them as the runner's own user, nor
against root through the runner's passwordless sudo. Such an upstream can
already forge its own row's result, as it could with a runner per row, so
sharing the runner adds no capability a deliberate attacker lacks there.
"""

